        "titulo": "When to use each greeting",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: «Hallo» and «Tschüs» are for friends and colleagues, at any time of day. STEP 2: «Guten Morgen» is said in the morning, «Guten Tag» during the day and «Guten Abend» in the evening, when you arrive. STEP 3: «Gute Nacht» is different. It is only said when parting, before someone goes to sleep. STEP 4: to ask how someone is, say «Wie geht’s?», read «vee gayts». Answer «Gut, danke!». STEP 5: «Danke» means thanks or thank you. «Bitte» does double duty: it means both please and you’re welcome.",
        "comparacao": "English makes a similar split: «good evening» to greet someone, «good night» only when saying goodbye or going to bed. German does the same with «Guten Abend» on arrival and «Gute Nacht» at bedtime. Be careful with «Guten Tag»: English speakers rarely say «good day», so do not translate it word for word. It works like a polite «hello» or «good afternoon» during the day.",
